1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,135
What would you do if someone offered

2
00:00:03,135 --> 00:00:05,056
you a barrel of oil,

3
00:00:05,056 --> 00:00:07,327
and paid you to take it?

4
00:00:07,583 --> 00:00:09,001
On April twentieth,

5
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:10,179
twenty twenty,

6
00:00:10,179 --> 00:00:12,943
that is exactly what happened.

7
00:00:13,173 --> 00:00:15,601
The price of American crude oil for

8
00:00:15,601 --> 00:00:18,490
delivery in May fell below zero,

9
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:20,432
for the first time since that contract

10
00:00:20,432 --> 00:00:23,703
began trading in nineteen eighty-three.

11
00:00:24,033 --> 00:00:25,605
At the end of the day it

12
00:00:25,605 --> 00:00:28,301
settled at minus thirty-seven dollars and

13
00:00:28,301 --> 00:00:30,473
sixty-three cents a barrel.

14
00:00:30,663 --> 00:00:32,963
People who owned oil were paying others

15
00:00:32,963 --> 00:00:34,943
to take it off their hands.

16
00:00:35,113 --> 00:00:36,593
To understand how,

17
00:00:36,593 --> 00:00:38,851
you have to understand that an oil

18
00:00:38,851 --> 00:00:40,833
price is really a promise,

19
00:00:40,833 --> 00:00:42,841
and on that day the promise came

20
00:00:42,841 --> 00:00:43,600
due.

21
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:50,967
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

22
00:01:02,217 --> 00:01:04,717
What happens to oil when the world

23
00:01:04,717 --> 00:01:06,255
stops moving?

24
00:01:11,217 --> 00:01:13,368
In the spring of twenty twenty,

25
00:01:13,368 --> 00:01:15,544
the pandemic shut down much of the

26
00:01:15,544 --> 00:01:16,607
planet.

27
00:01:16,607 --> 00:01:18,667
Planes stayed on the ground.

28
00:01:18,667 --> 00:01:20,307
Roads emptied.

29
00:01:20,307 --> 00:01:22,377
Factories went quiet.

30
00:01:22,577 --> 00:01:25,297
Analysts cited by the US Congressional

31
00:01:25,297 --> 00:01:27,791
Research Service estimated that global

32
00:01:27,791 --> 00:01:29,831
demand for crude oil fell by about

33
00:01:29,831 --> 00:01:31,848
thirty million barrels a day,

34
00:01:31,848 --> 00:01:34,568
around thirty percent of everything the

35
00:01:34,568 --> 00:01:36,302
world normally burns.

36
00:01:36,550 --> 00:01:39,330
The big producers tried to respond.

37
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:40,419
On April twelfth,

38
00:01:40,419 --> 00:01:42,420
the OPEC Plus group agreed to cut

39
00:01:42,420 --> 00:01:44,865
output by nine point seven million barrels

40
00:01:44,865 --> 00:01:45,510
a day,

41
00:01:45,510 --> 00:01:47,733
the largest single production cut in

42
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:49,000
history.

43
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:51,320
But it would only start in May.

44
00:01:51,430 --> 00:01:53,295
And even a record cut was much

45
00:01:53,295 --> 00:01:55,759
smaller than the hole in demand.

46
00:01:56,017 --> 00:01:58,327
Let me draw the problem.

47
00:01:58,787 --> 00:02:00,370
Here is the oil coming out of

48
00:02:00,370 --> 00:02:02,157
the ground every day.

49
00:02:02,447 --> 00:02:03,923
Here is the oil the world was

50
00:02:03,923 --> 00:02:05,577
actually using.

51
00:02:05,927 --> 00:02:09,497
The gap between them did not disappear.

52
00:02:09,737 --> 00:02:12,118
Every barrel nobody wanted had to go

53
00:02:12,118 --> 00:02:13,267
somewhere.

54
00:02:13,387 --> 00:02:14,463
Into a tank,

55
00:02:14,463 --> 00:02:15,538
a pipeline,

56
00:02:15,538 --> 00:02:16,627
or a ship.

57
00:02:16,627 --> 00:02:18,488
And the space in those tanks was

58
00:02:18,488 --> 00:02:19,886
running out.

59
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:23,028
To see why that mattered so much,

60
00:02:23,028 --> 00:02:25,062
you need to know how oil is

61
00:02:25,062 --> 00:02:26,825
really traded.

62
00:02:32,484 --> 00:02:34,804
When you buy oil on the market,

63
00:02:34,804 --> 00:02:37,228
what do you actually own?

64
00:02:41,484 --> 00:02:43,971
Most oil is traded through futures

65
00:02:43,971 --> 00:02:45,114
contracts.

66
00:02:45,114 --> 00:02:47,520
A futures contract is a promise to

67
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:49,271
buy or sell barrels of oil at

68
00:02:49,271 --> 00:02:50,124
a set price,

69
00:02:50,124 --> 00:02:52,474
on a set date in the future.

70
00:02:52,804 --> 00:02:55,588
The best known American contract is called

71
00:02:55,588 --> 00:02:56,884
W T I.

72
00:02:56,964 --> 00:02:58,773
Each month has its own contract,

73
00:02:58,773 --> 00:03:00,605
and each one ends on a fixed

74
00:03:00,605 --> 00:03:01,434
day.

75
00:03:01,594 --> 00:03:04,447
The May twenty twenty contract expired on

76
00:03:04,447 --> 00:03:06,236
April twenty-first.

77
00:03:06,484 --> 00:03:09,054
And here is the key.

78
00:03:09,144 --> 00:03:11,694
These contracts are for real oil.

79
00:03:11,784 --> 00:03:14,771
According to the US Energy Information

80
00:03:14,771 --> 00:03:16,126
Administration,

81
00:03:16,126 --> 00:03:18,653
anyone still holding one when it expires

82
00:03:18,653 --> 00:03:21,410
must make or take physical delivery of

83
00:03:21,410 --> 00:03:22,765
crude oil in Cushing,

84
00:03:22,765 --> 00:03:23,891
Oklahoma,

85
00:03:23,891 --> 00:03:25,499
a small town that is home to

86
00:03:25,499 --> 00:03:27,707
a major oil storage hub.

87
00:03:27,950 --> 00:03:30,560
Let me draw what that means.

88
00:03:30,670 --> 00:03:32,423
Here is a trader who bought a

89
00:03:32,423 --> 00:03:33,680
May contract.

90
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:35,597
Before the expiry date,

91
00:03:35,597 --> 00:03:37,410
they have two choices.

92
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:39,730
Sell the contract to someone else.

93
00:03:39,730 --> 00:03:40,604
Or keep it,

94
00:03:40,604 --> 00:03:42,620
and receive real barrels of oil in

95
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:43,718
Oklahoma,

96
00:03:43,718 --> 00:03:46,110
and find somewhere to put them.

97
00:03:46,290 --> 00:03:48,657
Most financial traders have no tanks and

98
00:03:48,657 --> 00:03:50,360
no pipelines.

99
00:03:50,360 --> 00:03:51,337
So for them,

100
00:03:51,337 --> 00:03:53,580
there is only one real choice.

101
00:03:53,580 --> 00:03:55,863
Sell before the deadline.

102
00:03:56,117 --> 00:03:58,747
Most people did leave in time.

103
00:03:58,857 --> 00:04:01,136
According to the US regulator,

104
00:04:01,136 --> 00:04:04,042
the Commodity Futures Trading Commission,

105
00:04:04,042 --> 00:04:06,132
the number of open May contracts fell

106
00:04:06,132 --> 00:04:08,223
from about six hundred and thirty-five

107
00:04:08,223 --> 00:04:10,104
thousand at the start of April to

108
00:04:10,104 --> 00:04:12,195
about one hundred and nine thousand on

109
00:04:12,195 --> 00:04:14,467
the morning of April twentieth.

110
00:04:14,667 --> 00:04:16,634
But that still left a lot of

111
00:04:16,634 --> 00:04:17,449
contracts,

112
00:04:17,449 --> 00:04:19,454
with one day to go.

113
00:04:19,717 --> 00:04:21,514
And to take delivery,

114
00:04:21,514 --> 00:04:23,627
you need one thing above all.

115
00:04:23,627 --> 00:04:25,967
Somewhere to put the oil.

116
00:04:31,617 --> 00:04:35,993
What happens when the tanks are full?

117
00:04:40,617 --> 00:04:43,096
The storage tanks at Cushing can hold

118
00:04:43,096 --> 00:04:46,402
about seventy-six million barrels of crude

119
00:04:46,402 --> 00:04:47,277
oil.

120
00:04:47,497 --> 00:04:48,442
In mid-March,

121
00:04:48,442 --> 00:04:50,497
they were about half full.

122
00:04:50,657 --> 00:04:52,482
By April seventeenth,

123
00:04:52,482 --> 00:04:55,197
they held sixty million barrels.

124
00:04:55,277 --> 00:04:57,805
They were seventy-six percent full,

125
00:04:57,805 --> 00:04:59,427
and filling fast.

126
00:04:59,827 --> 00:05:02,177
The three weeks to April seventeenth

127
00:05:02,177 --> 00:05:04,789
brought the three largest weekly increases

128
00:05:04,789 --> 00:05:06,694
ever recorded.

129
00:05:06,950 --> 00:05:09,804
And seventy-six percent full did not mean

130
00:05:09,804 --> 00:05:11,960
twenty-four percent free.

131
00:05:12,390 --> 00:05:15,108
As the Energy Information Administration

132
00:05:15,108 --> 00:05:15,671
noted,

133
00:05:15,671 --> 00:05:17,612
some of the empty space had probably

134
00:05:17,612 --> 00:05:20,136
already been leased or promised to someone

135
00:05:20,136 --> 00:05:20,930
else.

136
00:05:21,250 --> 00:05:23,130
For a trader looking for a tank

137
00:05:23,130 --> 00:05:24,362
at the last minute,

138
00:05:24,362 --> 00:05:26,424
there was almost nothing left.

139
00:05:26,684 --> 00:05:29,045
Oil companies started looking for storage

140
00:05:29,045 --> 00:05:30,741
anywhere they could find it,

141
00:05:30,741 --> 00:05:32,414
including at sea.

142
00:05:32,584 --> 00:05:34,583
In the first three weeks of April,

143
00:05:34,583 --> 00:05:36,604
the oil stored on tankers floating

144
00:05:36,604 --> 00:05:39,074
offshore rose by almost sixty-nine million

145
00:05:39,074 --> 00:05:39,725
barrels,

146
00:05:39,725 --> 00:05:42,196
to about one hundred and twenty-seven

147
00:05:42,196 --> 00:05:43,694
million barrels.

148
00:05:43,784 --> 00:05:46,463
Ships became floating warehouses.

149
00:05:46,717 --> 00:05:49,210
And the tanks kept filling after the

150
00:05:49,210 --> 00:05:50,087
crash.

151
00:05:50,287 --> 00:05:51,564
A week later,

152
00:05:51,564 --> 00:05:53,362
on April twenty-fourth,

153
00:05:53,362 --> 00:05:56,047
Cushing was eighty-one percent full.

154
00:05:56,287 --> 00:05:57,361
By the start of May,

155
00:05:57,361 --> 00:05:59,547
according to the Energy Information

156
00:05:59,547 --> 00:06:00,621
Administration,

157
00:06:00,621 --> 00:06:02,624
it had reached eighty-three percent of its

158
00:06:02,624 --> 00:06:04,317
working capacity.

159
00:06:04,637 --> 00:06:08,077
The hub was never completely full.

160
00:06:08,127 --> 00:06:10,618
But for anyone holding a May contract

161
00:06:10,618 --> 00:06:12,585
with nowhere to send the oil,

162
00:06:12,585 --> 00:06:14,679
it did not need to be.

163
00:06:14,917 --> 00:06:17,085
Let me draw what storage does to

164
00:06:17,085 --> 00:06:18,157
a price.

165
00:06:18,157 --> 00:06:18,972
Normally,

166
00:06:18,972 --> 00:06:21,063
a barrel today and a barrel next

167
00:06:21,063 --> 00:06:22,917
month cost about the same.

168
00:06:22,997 --> 00:06:24,506
But when storage runs out,

169
00:06:24,506 --> 00:06:27,130
oil for delivery right now becomes a

170
00:06:27,130 --> 00:06:28,087
burden.

171
00:06:28,087 --> 00:06:29,394
You have to pay someone to keep

172
00:06:29,394 --> 00:06:30,157
it.

173
00:06:30,167 --> 00:06:31,571
So the near month falls,

174
00:06:31,571 --> 00:06:33,837
and the later months hold up.

175
00:06:33,987 --> 00:06:35,604
The gap between them is the price

176
00:06:35,604 --> 00:06:36,810
of a tank.

177
00:06:37,050 --> 00:06:38,080
On Friday,

178
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:39,639
April seventeenth,

179
00:06:39,639 --> 00:06:42,017
the May contract closed at eighteen

180
00:06:42,017 --> 00:06:44,730
dollars and twenty-seven cents.

181
00:06:44,830 --> 00:06:46,926
Then came Monday.

182
00:06:52,584 --> 00:06:55,166
How can the price of something real

183
00:06:55,166 --> 00:06:57,200
fall below nothing?

184
00:07:01,584 --> 00:07:02,676
On Monday,

185
00:07:02,676 --> 00:07:04,049
April twentieth,

186
00:07:04,049 --> 00:07:07,383
the last full trading day before expiry,

187
00:07:07,383 --> 00:07:10,184
the May contract started falling and kept

188
00:07:10,184 --> 00:07:11,344
falling.

189
00:07:11,604 --> 00:07:14,261
Holders who could not take delivery needed

190
00:07:14,261 --> 00:07:15,304
to sell.

191
00:07:15,554 --> 00:07:17,079
But there were very few buyers with

192
00:07:17,079 --> 00:07:19,014
somewhere to put the oil.

193
00:07:19,314 --> 00:07:21,626
By the early afternoon in New York,

194
00:07:21,626 --> 00:07:23,922
the price was approaching zero.

195
00:07:24,184 --> 00:07:26,514
Then it went through the floor.

196
00:07:26,514 --> 00:07:28,703
According to the regulator,

197
00:07:28,703 --> 00:07:30,228
in twenty minutes,

198
00:07:30,228 --> 00:07:32,217
between eight minutes past two and

199
00:07:32,217 --> 00:07:33,964
twenty-eight minutes past two,

200
00:07:33,964 --> 00:07:35,953
the price fell from zero to minus

201
00:07:35,953 --> 00:07:38,654
thirty-nine dollars and fifty-five cents.

202
00:07:38,654 --> 00:07:41,270
A minute later it hit minus forty

203
00:07:41,270 --> 00:07:43,149
dollars and thirty-two cents,

204
00:07:43,149 --> 00:07:45,527
the lowest price in the history of

205
00:07:45,527 --> 00:07:46,841
the contract.

206
00:07:47,084 --> 00:07:48,106
At the close,

207
00:07:48,106 --> 00:07:50,463
the May contract settled at minus

208
00:07:50,463 --> 00:07:53,082
thirty-seven dollars and sixty-three

209
00:07:53,082 --> 00:07:53,944
cents.

210
00:07:54,254 --> 00:07:56,232
From the start of trading to the

211
00:07:56,232 --> 00:07:56,949
bottom,

212
00:07:56,949 --> 00:07:59,175
it had lost fifty-eight dollars and five

213
00:07:59,175 --> 00:08:00,764
cents a barrel.

214
00:08:01,044 --> 00:08:02,286
Some sellers were,

215
00:08:02,286 --> 00:08:03,275
in effect,

216
00:08:03,275 --> 00:08:05,810
paying a counterparty to let them out

217
00:08:05,810 --> 00:08:07,424
of their contracts.

218
00:08:07,684 --> 00:08:10,826
The strangest number was the gap between

219
00:08:10,826 --> 00:08:12,054
two months.

220
00:08:12,064 --> 00:08:13,003
On Friday,

221
00:08:13,003 --> 00:08:15,410
May oil had been about seven dollars

222
00:08:15,410 --> 00:08:17,214
cheaper than June oil.

223
00:08:17,414 --> 00:08:18,785
By Monday's close,

224
00:08:18,785 --> 00:08:21,344
the gap was fifty-eight dollars.

225
00:08:21,394 --> 00:08:22,517
The June contract,

226
00:08:22,517 --> 00:08:24,809
for the same oil delivered just one

227
00:08:24,809 --> 00:08:25,703
month later,

228
00:08:25,703 --> 00:08:28,225
closed at twenty dollars and forty-three

229
00:08:28,225 --> 00:08:29,054
cents.

230
00:08:29,174 --> 00:08:31,804
Nothing had happened to the oil itself.

231
00:08:31,874 --> 00:08:33,665
Only to the date.

232
00:08:33,917 --> 00:08:36,137
Let me draw that Monday.

233
00:08:36,177 --> 00:08:38,834
Here is the May contract on Friday,

234
00:08:38,834 --> 00:08:40,507
eighteen dollars.

235
00:08:40,637 --> 00:08:43,150
Here is the June contract on Monday,

236
00:08:43,150 --> 00:08:45,527
still around twenty dollars.

237
00:08:45,577 --> 00:08:47,525
And here is the May contract at

238
00:08:47,525 --> 00:08:48,232
the close,

239
00:08:48,232 --> 00:08:51,267
almost thirty-eight dollars below zero.

240
00:08:51,437 --> 00:08:52,525
The same oil,

241
00:08:52,525 --> 00:08:54,171
the same pipeline,

242
00:08:54,171 --> 00:08:55,887
one month apart.

243
00:08:55,997 --> 00:08:59,037
All the other months stayed above zero.

244
00:08:59,267 --> 00:09:01,575
Only the one that was about to

245
00:09:01,575 --> 00:09:03,457
arrive collapsed.

246
00:09:03,717 --> 00:09:04,837
The next morning,

247
00:09:04,837 --> 00:09:07,123
the world woke up to headlines about

248
00:09:07,123 --> 00:09:08,637
negative oil.

249
00:09:08,777 --> 00:09:11,259
But the story was not over.

250
00:09:16,917 --> 00:09:19,445
Who was left holding the oil?

251
00:09:25,917 --> 00:09:27,864
On April twenty-first,

252
00:09:27,864 --> 00:09:28,964
its last day,

253
00:09:28,964 --> 00:09:31,786
the May contract settled at ten dollars

254
00:09:31,786 --> 00:09:32,886
and one cent,

255
00:09:32,886 --> 00:09:34,897
positive again.

256
00:09:35,037 --> 00:09:36,939
But the panic spread to the next

257
00:09:36,939 --> 00:09:37,777
month.

258
00:09:37,997 --> 00:09:40,351
The June contract fell by forty-three

259
00:09:40,351 --> 00:09:41,632
percent that day,

260
00:09:41,632 --> 00:09:45,371
to eleven dollars and fifty-seven cents.

261
00:09:45,617 --> 00:09:47,777
The regulator later looked at who was

262
00:09:47,777 --> 00:09:48,737
trading.

263
00:09:48,847 --> 00:09:50,373
On April twentieth,

264
00:09:50,373 --> 00:09:52,700
out of four hundred and forty-eight large

265
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:54,225
traders it tracked,

266
00:09:54,225 --> 00:09:56,811
one hundred and seventy-three still held

267
00:09:56,811 --> 00:09:58,187
May contracts.

268
00:09:58,337 --> 00:10:00,784
Its interim report described the

269
00:10:00,784 --> 00:10:02,473
conditions in detail,

270
00:10:02,473 --> 00:10:04,431
but did not name a single cause

271
00:10:04,431 --> 00:10:05,765
for the crash.

272
00:10:06,017 --> 00:10:09,187
The exchange had seen the risk coming.

273
00:10:09,387 --> 00:10:10,759
In early April,

274
00:10:10,759 --> 00:10:13,085
CME Group sent notices to its members

275
00:10:13,085 --> 00:10:16,550
about the possibility of negative prices,

276
00:10:16,550 --> 00:10:19,108
and opened a testing system so that

277
00:10:19,108 --> 00:10:20,736
trading firms could check that their

278
00:10:20,736 --> 00:10:24,127
computers could handle a price below zero.

279
00:10:24,687 --> 00:10:26,846
In almost forty years of trading,

280
00:10:26,846 --> 00:10:29,409
that had never happened before.

281
00:10:29,650 --> 00:10:32,303
As lockdowns eased and demand slowly

282
00:10:32,303 --> 00:10:33,338
returned,

283
00:10:33,338 --> 00:10:35,530
the price recovered.

284
00:10:35,590 --> 00:10:36,686
By July first,

285
00:10:36,686 --> 00:10:39,145
a barrel of American oil was back

286
00:10:39,145 --> 00:10:41,310
at about forty dollars.

287
00:10:41,510 --> 00:10:44,911
Prices below zero had not even lasted

288
00:10:44,911 --> 00:10:46,645
two trading days.

289
00:10:46,884 --> 00:10:49,242
So what does negative oil teach about

290
00:10:49,242 --> 00:10:50,314
prices?

291
00:10:50,324 --> 00:10:51,824
That a price is not just about

292
00:10:51,824 --> 00:10:53,924
how useful something is.

293
00:10:54,024 --> 00:10:56,112
It is also about where it is,

294
00:10:56,112 --> 00:10:57,357
when it arrives,

295
00:10:57,357 --> 00:10:59,644
and whether you have room for it.

296
00:10:59,844 --> 00:11:01,436
On April twentieth,

297
00:11:01,436 --> 00:11:03,924
the oil had not lost its value.

298
00:11:03,994 --> 00:11:05,649
The world had simply run out of

299
00:11:05,649 --> 00:11:07,284
places to put it.

300
00:11:07,554 --> 00:11:09,248
And a contract that looked like a

301
00:11:09,248 --> 00:11:10,941
piece of paper turned out to be

302
00:11:10,941 --> 00:11:13,387
a promise to take delivery of something

303
00:11:13,387 --> 00:11:14,552
very real.

304
00:11:14,817 --> 00:11:16,970
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

305
00:11:16,970 --> 00:11:19,537
we follow the money behind another hidden

306
00:11:19,537 --> 00:11:21,717
link of the world economy.

307
00:11:21,977 --> 00:11:24,405
This is The Hidden Economics.
